    "q-93611": {
      "dom_id": "step-q93611",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $-10$ is a natural number\n\nII. $-10$ is an integer\n\nIII. $-10$ is a rational number",
      "worked_solution": "Let's recall our definitions.\n\n- The natural numbers consist of all positive whole numbers: $1,\\,2,\\,3,\\,4,\\,5,…$\n\n- The integers consist of all of the positive and negative whole numbers, and zero: $…,-3,\\,-2,\\,-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…$\n\n- The rationals consist of all numbers that can be written as the ratio of two integers.\n\nNow, let's go through each number in turn.\n\n- $-10$ is not a natural number.\n\n- $-10$ is an integer.\n\n- $-10$ is a rational number because it can be written as $\\frac{(-10)}{1}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"II and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-93611-a",
              "type": "text",
              "value": "III only",
              "html": "III only"
            },
            {
              "option": "b",
